      "transcript": "Ailsa Trenwith: Welcome to the Harbour Lantern Night Market induction. I am Ailsa, the site coordinator. Most arrangements are straightforward, but the waterfront lane is narrow, and a busy evening depends on everybody following the same sequence.\nAilsa Trenwith: Your coloured pass shows an unloading time. Please keep to it, not because the pitch changes or the parking is cheaper, but because one shared lane must keep moving.\nAilsa Trenwith: Food and craft stalls use the same unloading entrance. Marshals will direct vans forward; reversing is allowed only when a marshal signals. After unloading, vehicles move to the remote car park, leaving fire access clear.\nAilsa Trenwith: At the power desk, staff do not count appliances or judge cable colour. They check that each powered item carries a current power tag before it is connected.\nAilsa Trenwith: Hygiene controls are divided by task. Hand-washing units are for vendors handling food. Drinking-water taps are marked blue, and cleaning water is collected separately. These are practical site arrangements rather than optional conveniences.\nAilsa Trenwith: The grey station is for tied bags of general non-food rubbish. Cardboard goes to the reuse cage, while cooking oil has its own sealed return point.\nAilsa Trenwith: Power is supplied from covered distribution boxes. Domestic multi-plugs are not accepted. If you have no electrical equipment, you still pass the desk so staff can mark that status on the site list.\nAilsa Trenwith: At nine forty-five we begin the quiet close. Trading continues for fifteen minutes, but amplified music stops so nearby residents are not disturbed.\nAilsa Trenwith: Waste becomes busiest near closing. Separate bins reduce spills and let the cleaning crew work without crossing the customer queue. A steward can show you the station, but cannot carry hot oil from a stall.\nAilsa Trenwith: A vehicle pass only admits you to unloading, and prices are your responsibility. The laminated stall permit must remain visible throughout the evening.\nAilsa Trenwith: For departure, keep products on display until ten. Pack inside your pitch first, then fetch your vehicle when your row is called. That staged exit is quieter and leaves the emergency lane usable.\nAilsa Trenwith: Once unloaded, collapse empty delivery crates and take them back to the loading bay. Do not leave them behind your stall.\nAilsa Trenwith: If an extension lead is split or its tag cannot be read, carry it unplugged to the power desk and report it there.\nAilsa Trenwith: Tie ordinary rubbish securely. Those bags belong at the grey waste station, not at the cardboard cage beside it.\nAilsa Trenwith: Let used oil cool in its original covered container. Return it only at the oil return point after the dinner rush.\nAilsa Trenwith: If wind damages your permit or you misplace it, the permit office can verify your booking and print a replacement.\nAilsa Trenwith: Stall lighting may be switched on after the power check, but decorative lights must stay within the pitch. Battery lights are acceptable if they are stable and do not obstruct the public aisle. Gas equipment is handled under a separate safety briefing. During trading, keep stock boxes under the table rather than in the lane. A market steward may ask you to move an item even if it fitted during setup, because customer flow changes after dark. The first-aid point is near the permit office, while lost property goes to the information tent. Those facilities are not part of the matching task, but you should know them before customers arrive. At closing, do not begin vehicle loading until your row number is announced. This avoids a queue across the pedestrian exit.\nAilsa Trenwith: Please ask location questions now. Individual trading advice can be discussed with the permit team after this shared briefing.",
