      "instructions": "21-25: Choose the correct letter, A, B or C.\n26-30: Choose the correct letter for each item.",
      "transcript": "Finlay Rusk: Marta, the tutor liked our question about habitat, but asked whether our method separates habitat effects from conditions on the sampling day. We need a design another group could repeat, not simply a longer species list.\nMarta Keir: Our first plan compares the woodland and sports field as if habitat is the only difference, but the woodland plots are much wetter. Soil moisture could explain a count difference.\nFinlay Rusk: The campus has six potential plots in each habitat. Random selection sounds attractive, but a completely random draw could give us all wet woodland plots and all dry grass plots. A blocking variable would make the comparison more informative.\nMarta Keir: I considered hand sorting alone, yet effort would vary. We should retain mustard extraction because we can apply the same concentration, area and waiting time at every plot.\nFinlay Rusk: We also need one defined sampling quadrat. Enlarging it would produce more organisms but would not remove bias. Replicating several modest quadrats is preferable because one unusually rich patch will not dominate the whole result.\nMarta Keir: For observer bias, swapping guides will not help. The same pair of people should sort every sample, then resolve disagreements together.\nFinlay Rusk: Extraction choice matters because methods sample behaviour as well as abundance. Our report should say that clearly. We are not claiming one method captures every earthworm equally; we are keeping effort repeatable for this comparison.\nMarta Keir: Before any solution is poured, we will record soil moisture. Worm length and vegetation height can be secondary, but moisture must come first.\nMarta Keir: Let us allocate tasks without creating different procedures. I can prepare coded sample bags and the moisture probe. Finlay can mark coordinates, but we will both do the sorting so the decision rule stays constant.\nFinlay Rusk: So the primary comparison will use paired moist plots, one in each habitat. Total habitat counts and time-of-day patterns can remain exploratory.\nMarta Keir: The data sheet needs zeroes as well as positive counts. A blank cell might mean nobody looked. A recorded zero means the method was completed and no worm emerged during the timed interval.\nFinlay Rusk: Recent rainfall creates unequal moisture. Pairing plots by their measured moisture is the revision that addresses that problem.\nMarta Keir: Finally, the report should distinguish observations from interpretation. Moisture, counts and photographs are observations. Any explanation about habitat quality comes later and must acknowledge weather and limited sample size.\nFinlay Rusk: If one team waits five minutes and another waits ten, effort changes. We will standardise extraction time with a single timer.\nMarta Keir: Juvenile worms are hard to identify consistently. A shared identification check by both students should reduce disagreement.\nFinlay Rusk: When a specimen remains uncertain, we will photograph it against a scale and confirm it later rather than inventing a species label.\nMarta Keir: To prevent trampling from changing the surface, we will measure each quadrat before sampling and approach from the marked path.\nFinlay Rusk: We also need a rule for weather interruption. If rain begins after the first quadrat, we should finish that paired plot but not start a new pair under different conditions. The sheet can mark the interruption rather than hiding it. Replication must be balanced across the two habitats: three paired plots on the first morning and three on the second, with the order alternated. That prevents one habitat always being sampled after the sun has warmed the soil. For specimen handling, adults can be grouped provisionally in the field, but final labels are assigned only after the shared check. Juveniles remain in a separate category because forcing a species name would create false precision. We should return organisms near the sampling point after identification, following the approved field procedure. The report will describe that handling without claiming the method is harmless in every circumstance. Finally, raw photographs should use coded plot numbers, not student names, so the archive can be checked by the tutor without exposing unnecessary personal information.\nMarta Keir: We can now rewrite the methods section and bring the paired-plot plan to the tutor tomorrow.",
