      "instructions": "31-36: Complete the notes. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.\n37-40: Answer the questions. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.",
      "transcript": "Lecturer: Today we move from the basic idea of echolocation to a harder environment: flight among vegetation. The important point is not that clutter has one universal definition. Researchers can measure it in several ways, and each measure captures a different aspect of spatial complexity.\nLecturer: Many bats emit a downward frequency sweep, so a single brief call carries a broad range of frequencies rather than one unchanging note.\nLecturer: A call begins as a pressure wave produced in the vocal system. After it leaves the bat, surfaces reflect some energy. The animal does not receive a labelled message; its nervous system must compare timing, frequency and intensity across rapidly changing echoes.\nLecturer: The simplest ranging cue is echo delay: the interval between the outgoing call and its return increases with target distance.\nLecturer: Frequency matters because a broad signal can provide fine information about a target. Yet high frequencies also lose energy quickly in air. A bat therefore balances detail against range, and the balance changes as it moves from open space towards leaves.\nLecturer: In the outer ear, a small projection called the tragus contributes cues that help the auditory system analyse returning sound.\nLecturer: Timing is equally important. If a new call is made before the previous echoes have been processed, signals may overlap. Close to an obstacle, shorter intervals update the scene rapidly; farther away, longer intervals leave time for distant returns.\nLecturer: During flight, call production may be coordinated with each wingbeat, which can reduce the energetic cost of breathing and calling.\nLecturer: The ears are not passive funnels. Their shape affects how sound reaches the eardrum, and movements can alter the reception pattern. This contributes to localisation, especially when the animal changes direction between one call and the next.\nLecturer: Branches and leaves make what researchers call a cluttered habitat, where echoes arrive from many surfaces in very quick succession.\nLecturer: Now consider prey capture. A small insect may return a weak echo while a broad leaf sends back a strong one. Differences in movement and acoustic texture can help, but the task becomes more demanding when wind moves the vegetation too.\nLecturer: When a bat approaches vegetation, shorter calls reduce overlap between an outgoing signal and the earliest returning echoes.\nLecturer: Field studies connect call behaviour with mapped habitat. Some count openings, some estimate the distance to the nearest vegetation, and others reconstruct three-dimensional structure. Results should be interpreted in relation to the chosen clutter measure.\nLecturer: One practical clutter measure is to count vegetation gaps along a defined line, although other optical and spatial measures are possible.\nLecturer: The ecological conclusion is therefore a trade-off, not a perfect solution. Signals adapted for rapid close-range decisions may not travel far. Signals that reach farther may provide updates too slowly for dense branches. Bats adjust rather than use one fixed call.\nLecturer: To detect an insect near foliage, the bat must separate the prey echo from strong background echoes returned by leaves and stems.\nLecturer: Small ear movement can change the direction from which sound is received most strongly, adding dynamic information during approach.\nLecturer: In dense vegetation, the chief benefit of brief calls is rapid updates about nearby obstacles. Their lower energy can shorten range, but that is a cost rather than the benefit.\nLecturer: Call shape also reflects the task. During search, a bat may leave longer gaps and listen over a broader area. During approach, calls become more frequent as distance closes. This sequence gives researchers behavioural phases, but boundaries are gradual rather than perfectly discrete. Microphones introduce their own sampling limits. A detector placed beside a path may record bats that use open gaps more readily than individuals moving deep within foliage. Comparing call counts without considering placement could therefore exaggerate one strategy. Researchers sometimes pair acoustic recordings with thermal cameras or small movement tags. Each instrument adds information but also changes the sample: tags can be attached only to captured animals, while cameras cover a limited field of view. The term clutter should consequently be operational. Counting gaps, estimating nearest-obstacle distance and measuring vegetation density are related approaches, not interchangeable numbers. When a study reports an association between one measure and call duration, the result belongs to that defined measure and setting. This caution does not weaken the research; it makes comparisons reproducible. Echolocation is impressive precisely because the animal integrates incomplete echoes while moving in real time, not because every echo yields an exact three-dimensional map.\nLecturer: In the next lecture we will compare these acoustic strategies with vision and touch during the final stage of prey capture.",
