      "passage": "A. A freight container looks deliberately unremarkable, yet its value lies in agreement. Standard dimensions and corner fittings allow compatible equipment to lift the same box between ship, train and lorry. Cargo can remain inside while responsibility for movement passes from one operator to another. This reduces repeated handling, but it should not be confused with effortless transport. Intermodal transfer works because vehicles, cranes, yards, documents and safety rules have been adjusted to recognise the same physical unit.\n\nB. It is tempting to credit the box itself with transforming world trade. That story is too neat. Containers delivered large gains only where ports deepened berths, installed cranes, reorganised storage and connected efficiently to inland routes. Shipping companies also had to acquire suitable vessels, while exporters altered packing and scheduling. Standardisation coordinated these investments; it did not replace them. The distinction matters because it prevents an object from receiving sole credit for a change produced by an entire system.\n\nC. Shared dimensions solve a compatibility problem, not a structural one. A damaged box can endanger workers and cargo even if it fits every crane. International coordination therefore includes safety and inspection requirements as well as measurements. An inspection regime makes visible who must check condition and when. This adds time and paperwork, but it protects the assumption on which rapid handling depends: equipment can treat a container as a known unit only when its structure remains trustworthy.\n\nD. Benefits were uneven. A port with capital, land and strong inland connections could process high volumes, whereas a smaller harbour might lose traffic when it could not justify specialised infrastructure. Workers whose skills centred on manual cargo handling faced disruption, while other roles expanded around cranes, logistics and maintenance. These outcomes do not show that standards failed. They show that a common interface changes bargaining power and location as well as technical efficiency. Uniform equipment can produce highly non-uniform social effects.\n\nE. Once many firms have invested in compatible assets, changing the standard becomes difficult. This is infrastructure lock-in: not an absolute prohibition on innovation, but a rising cost of departure. A new design may be technically superior and still struggle if it requires different cranes, storage slots and vehicle frames. The installed system gives current dimensions a practical authority beyond the wording of any rule. Stability supports confidence, yet it can also preserve compromises chosen under earlier conditions.\n\nF. A container standard does not decide which goods should move, how emissions should be priced or whether a route is socially desirable. Those questions remain outside the box. Faster transfer can lower one cost while encouraging more distant production and additional transport. The author’s point is not that coordination is harmful, but that efficiency at an interface is only one layer of evaluation. Policy must still consider external costs, labour transitions and the resilience of long supply chains.\n\nG. Lock-in is not paralysis. Operators can introduce tracking devices, improved seals or new inspection methods without discarding the entire interface. Such incremental change is attractive because it preserves compatibility while improving information or safety. More radical alteration is possible when benefits are large enough and transition arrangements are shared. The crucial variable is coordination: a single carrier cannot redesign a network alone, but groups can change it when the costs and timetable are made explicit.\n\nH. The container is best understood as an institutional object. Its steel walls carry goods, while its standard form carries commitments among organisations that may never meet. Those commitments reduce uncertainty at each transfer and make large-scale scheduling possible. They also distribute dependence: a delay in one port can propagate through many routes. The achievement of standardisation is therefore neither the triumph of a clever box nor a tale of rigid uniformity. It is the construction of a shared platform whose benefits and constraints must be governed over time.\n\nI. Standardisation can increase resilience by making equipment and routes substitutable, but it can also concentrate dependence. When many supply chains rely on a small number of compatible hubs, disruption at one hub may affect distant firms. This is not an argument for abandoning shared standards. It is a reminder that compatibility and redundancy are different properties. A network may transfer containers efficiently in normal conditions yet have few alternatives during a closure. Planners who value resilience might preserve spare capacity, diversify inland connections or agree temporary procedures before a crisis. Such measures sit outside the steel box, but they determine whether the standardised system bends or breaks under unusual pressure.",
